The moment runs. Stopping here as instructed.

## What the moment is

**The size decision on the shop floor** — the one thing a photograph of a clothing site cannot show. Nine garments, each one a panel of its own cloth. Pressing a size does three things at once, all from one value: the cloth **pulls back** (a bigger size is literally more cloth in frame), a **tape rule** laid across the bottom of the cloth extends to that size's chest measurement, and the chest/waist/sleeve numbers **count** to it. Sold-out sizes are struck through, refuse the press with a spring-back, and say why. Adding to the bag **cuts a swatch of that cloth and carries it** into the bag, where it lands as that line's thumbnail; the subtotal counts up and the £100 free-delivery threshold is drawn on the same rule, filling as you approach it.

One measuring language throughout: cloth, a rule across it, a number at the end of the rule. That is the shop's actual pitch — fabric weights and body measurements — given a form.

## What material it moves

Nine real fabric macros, on disk at `public/cloth/`, all **CC0** (rawpixel/StockSnap via the Openverse API), each matched to the fabric statement the copy makes — the white cotton oxford weave, a 240gsm jersey loop, indigo canvas, brushed grey wool with a button, brown wool twill, a denim waistband with its rivet and selvedge stitching, navy lambswool rib, a stack of camel/oat drape, washed slub twill. Not downloads: each was cropped to a 4:5 crop family, graded to one temperature and contrast so the nine read as one shoot, and emitted as webp derivatives at two widths in the same pass. Sources and licences are in `public/cloth/CREDITS.txt`.

## What drives it

GSAP 3.15, installed for this. Each card tweens **one authored value** — a fractional index into the real size chart — and an `onUpdate` writes the panel zoom, the rule span, and every number from it, so the cloth and the numbers can never disagree. The bag runs the same way off the subtotal. The add-to-bag flight is a measured FLIP between the panel's rect and the bag's. Reduced motion sets every value instantly instead of switching the mechanism off. Underneath it, the interaction baseline: designed hover/focus/press/disabled on every chip, filter, button and remove link.

Verified running at 1440 and 390 in Chromium against `npm run preview`: filter counts and the All state, per-garment sold-out enforcement, size→cloth→numbers, add, remove, subtotal, the free-delivery gap. `npm run build` succeeds. Nothing committed.

## What I could not get

**Photographs of the garments themselves.** I searched Openverse (144 candidates over 12 queries), Wikimedia Commons (56), and Unsplash's API — and read all three as contact sheets. Openverse returned amateur snapshots of other labels' branded clothing; Commons returned museum portraits and retail-shelf photos with price tags; Unsplash and Pexels both need an API key I don't have. Putting another brand's shirt on this page would be a fabricated claim, so I did not. The cloth is the honest rung available, and it is genuinely this shop's subject — but a buyer cannot yet see the shape of the garment, only its fabric. **If a Pexels/Unsplash key or a real product shoot can be supplied, that is the single biggest upgrade available to this page**, and the card is built so the panel can take a garment photo without changing the mechanism.

Also not verified: reduced motion under browser emulation (the code path exists and the CSS guard is in; I couldn't toggle the media feature in this harness). And a full-page screenshot at 1440 drops the offscreen cloth images — a Chrome capture artifact, not a page defect; the viewport captures show them all painting.

Not built, per the checkpoint: the size chart section, care, shipping and returns, reviews, the three shop facts, and the hero/about. Their content is untouched in `src/App.jsx`.